### What is the 0/1 Knapsack pattern?

The 0/1 Knapsack pattern applies whenever you face a set of items, each with a cost and a benefit, and a total budget constraint. Each item is either selected or not: there is no partial selection. You want to maximise (or minimise) the total benefit within the budget.

This pattern is one of the most important in dynamic programming interviews. Many problems reduce to it: subset sum (can you reach a target weight?), partition equal subset (can you split items into two equal-sum groups?), target sum (count subsets that sum to a target), and bounded counting problems.

```java
package com.bank.dsa;

public class KnapsackPattern {

    public static int maxProfit(int[] weight, int[] profit, int capacity) {
        int n = weight.length;
        int[][] dp = new int[n + 1][capacity + 1];

        for (int i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
            for (int c = 0; c <= capacity; c++) {
                dp[i][c] = dp[i - 1][c];  // exclude
                if (weight[i - 1] <= c) {
                    dp[i][c] = Math.max(dp[i][c],
                        profit[i - 1] + dp[i - 1][c - weight[i - 1]]);  // include
                }
            }
        }
        return dp[n][capacity];
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] w = {1, 2, 3, 5};
        int[] p = {1, 6, 10, 16};
        System.out.println("Max profit: " + maxProfit(w, p, 7));
    }
}
```

```text
Max profit: 22
```

### Why does the 0/1 Knapsack pattern exist?

Resource allocation under constraints is everywhere in business and computing. A bank decides which loan applications to fund within a quarterly capital budget. A project manager selects features to build within a sprint's capacity. A container packer decides which packages to load within a weight limit. All of these are include-or-exclude decisions under a shared constraint, which is the 0/1 Knapsack pattern.

### How does the 2D table work internally?

The table has (N + 1) rows and (C + 1) columns. Row zero represents zero items (all values zero). Column zero represents zero capacity (all values zero). Cell dp[i][c] stores the maximum profit using the first i items with capacity c.

For each item i at each capacity c, there are two choices:
- **Exclude item i**: dp[i][c] = dp[i-1][c], the best value without this item.
- **Include item i** (if weight[i] <= c): dp[i][c] = profit[i] + dp[i-1][c - weight[i]], the item's profit plus the best value with the remaining capacity from the previous items.

We take the maximum of the two. The answer is dp[N][C].

```java
package com.bank.dsa;

public class KnapsackTablePrint {

    public static void printTable(int[] w, int[] p, int cap) {
        int n = w.length;
        int[][] dp = new int[n + 1][cap + 1];
        for (int i = 1; i <= n; i++)
            for (int c = 0; c <= cap; c++) {
                dp[i][c] = dp[i - 1][c];
                if (w[i - 1] <= c)
                    dp[i][c] = Math.max(dp[i][c], p[i - 1] + dp[i - 1][c - w[i - 1]]);
            }

        System.out.print("     ");
        for (int c = 0; c <= cap; c++) System.out.printf("%4d", c);
        System.out.println();
        for (int i = 0; i <= n; i++) {
            System.out.printf("i=%d: ", i);
            for (int c = 0; c <= cap; c++) System.out.printf("%4d", dp[i][c]);
            System.out.println();
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        printTable(new int[]{1, 3, 4, 5}, new int[]{1, 4, 5, 7}, 7);
    }
}
```

