### How do you optimise space to one row?

Since row i depends only on row i-1, we collapse the table to a single array. For each item, we sweep capacity from high to low, updating dp[c] with the maximum of its current value and profit[i] + dp[c - weight[i]]. The downward sweep ensures dp[c - weight[i]] has not been updated yet for the current item.

```java
package com.bank.dsa;

public class KnapsackOneRow {

    public static int maxProfit(int[] weight, int[] profit, int capacity) {
        int[] dp = new int[capacity + 1];
        for (int i = 0; i < weight.length; i++) {
            for (int c = capacity; c >= weight[i]; c--) {
                dp[c] = Math.max(dp[c], profit[i] + dp[c - weight[i]]);
            }
        }
        return dp[capacity];
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(maxProfit(new int[]{1, 3, 4, 5}, new int[]{1, 4, 5, 7}, 7));
    }
}
```

```text
9
```

### How is 0/1 Knapsack different from fractional knapsack?

| Aspect | 0/1 Knapsack | Fractional Knapsack |
|---|---|---|
| Selection | Item taken fully or not at all | Can take a fraction of an item |
| Algorithm | Dynamic programming | Greedy (sort by value/weight ratio) |
| Time | O(N * C) pseudo-polynomial | O(N log N) for sorting |
| Optimal guarantee | Always optimal | Always optimal |
| Use case | Loan deals (fund fully or skip) | Buying partial shares of a stock |

```java
package com.bank.dsa;

import java.util.Arrays;

public class FractionalKnapsack {

    public static double maxValue(int[] weight, int[] value, int capacity) {
        int n = weight.length;
        Integer[] idx = new Integer[n];
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) idx[i] = i;
        Arrays.sort(idx, (a, b) -> Double.compare(
            (double) value[b] / weight[b], (double) value[a] / weight[a]));

        double total = 0;
        int remaining = capacity;
        for (int i : idx) {
            if (remaining == 0) break;
            int take = Math.min(weight[i], remaining);
            total += (double) value[i] * take / weight[i];
            remaining -= take;
        }
        return total;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.printf("Fractional: %.2f%n",
            maxValue(new int[]{1, 3, 4, 5}, new int[]{1, 4, 5, 7}, 7));
    }
}
```

```text
Fractional: 11.33
```

### What are common mistakes?

The most common mistake is sweeping capacity upward in the 1D version, which silently turns it into unbounded knapsack.

```java
// WRONG: upward sweep allows item reuse
for (int c = weight[i]; c <= capacity; c++)
    dp[c] = Math.max(dp[c], profit[i] + dp[c - weight[i]]);

// CORRECT: downward sweep ensures each item used once
for (int c = capacity; c >= weight[i]; c--)
    dp[c] = Math.max(dp[c], profit[i] + dp[c - weight[i]]);
```

Another mistake is using 1-based item indexing in the profit and weight arrays without adjusting the array access, causing `ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException`.

### What are the edge cases?

When all items are heavier than the capacity, the answer is zero. When capacity is zero, the answer is zero. When a single item fits, the answer is that item's profit.

```java
package com.bank.dsa;

public class KnapsackEdgeCases {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(KnapsackOneRow.maxProfit(new int[]{10}, new int[]{5}, 3));   // 0
        System.out.println(KnapsackOneRow.maxProfit(new int[]{3}, new int[]{5}, 3));    // 5
        System.out.println(KnapsackOneRow.maxProfit(new int[]{}, new int[]{}, 10));     // 0
    }
}
```

```text
0
5
0
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

A senior engineer should know that 0/1 Knapsack is NP-hard in general, and the O(N * C) algorithm is pseudo-polynomial because it depends on the numeric value of C. When C is enormous, the meet-in-the-middle approach splits items into two halves, enumerates all subsets of each in O(2^(N/2)), and combines them.

The pattern extends to multi-dimensional knapsacks (multiple constraints like weight and volume), which add dimensions to the DP table. It also connects to branch-and-bound solvers used in operations research for real-world optimisation.

In Java, bitset tricks can speed up subset-sum variants of knapsack. Using `BitSet` and shifting, you can process all capacities in parallel with a single bitwise OR operation per item.
